Z||000000||bwaits||05-21-2000||09:32 PM||||I am looking into building a fiero for road racing. The class will be Improved Touring with the SCCA club. It will be classified with Honda crx and the like. The car is classified in 2 configurations:<BR>1) 85-87 2.8 GT<BR>2) 88 GT, Formula<BR>The 88 has to weigh more by the rules than the 85-87. <BR>My questions are:<P>If you were going to choose, which one would you and why?<BR>How much did each of these weigh from the factory?<BR>What are the benefits to the 88 formula?<BR>Is the 2.8 the same as the cavalier 2.8, if not what are the differences.<BR>Are there any website with this kind of information?<P>-bwaits<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||05-21-2000||10:24 PM|||| I would personally go for the pre-88 notchback models simply because the aftermarket for the suspension goodies is cheaper and larger.  Also, the notchbacks lighter than the fastbacks.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||05-21-2000||10:28 PM||||Just for your refrence, my '87 GT weighs 2700lbs bone stock.  Fairly hefty for such a small car. The 2.8 is the high-output model.  Basically it's higher compression and larger valves (140hp 170ft lbs of torque.)||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||05-21-2000||10:49 PM||||I'd go with a  notchback too.. the GTs look nice but the weight issue.. I'll sell ya my car! Has ALL the mods you'd ever want,except a Kirkey seat and Cage. and R compound tires. (keeping my A032s)I have over 10K in recipts and willing to part with it for FAR less.   (I want a White automatic GT)  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||bwaits||05-21-2000||11:03 PM||||The 87 GT can be taken down to 2560 pounds.<BR>The 88 has to weigh in at 2780 pounds.<BR>This is with driver at the end of the race.<BR>I am 120# or so. That leaves 2450 for 85-87<BR>and 2660 for the 88. Why would they put more weight on the 88. I am not sure you could get the 85-87 down to weight.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||bwaits||05-21-2000||11:08 PM||||what is this "junior member" next to username?||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT Bastard||05-21-2000||11:21 PM||||it'll go away after you've posted a few times.||||reg||1
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||05-21-2000||11:34 PM||||The 88 is the way to go. It has suspension goemetry far superior to that of the pre 88s. No matter what you do to the earlier models the goemetry will not change. The difference is evident at high speeds... I have owned an 86SE and now have an 88 with a V6. The pre 88s have more available for them, but they need a considerable amount of money put into them just to bring them up to par with the 88s. I can't think of anything that is not available for the 88 that is for the other years come to think of it. At high speeds the 88 has almost no bump steer (I can't feel any anyway). <P>I can't figure out why the notchback weighs less. When I put the fastback clip on my car it felt about the same weight as the notchback clip. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||George Ryan||05-21-2000||11:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bwaits:<BR><B>The 87 GT can be taken down to 2560 pounds.<BR>The 88 has to weigh in at 2780 pounds.<BR>This is with driver at the end of the race.<BR>I am 120# or so. That leaves 2450 for 85-87<BR>and 2660 for the 88. Why would they put more weight on the 88. I am not sure you could get the 85-87 down to weight.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Just for info, my racing Fiero weighs in at 1980 (without driver) up due to penalty weight from 1900lbs.<P>G<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||1FST2M6||05-22-2000||11:45 AM||||my car weighs in at about that i'll check my print out tonite... (at home-I'm at work.. workin hard can't ya tell) No A/C or any of it components, anything that doesn't make it turn or go fast came off the car... well i put a radio back in it after 8 months of no tunes... figured 10lbs i could deal with. Not sure either why the GT weights more but it does... anyone with an idea? ||||reg||1
Z||000010||87PegasusGT||05-22-2000||01:15 PM||||What are some easy weight reductions that let me keep amenities i.e. ac, power everything, etc?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Carrolles||05-23-2000||07:55 AM||||If you pull the power steering out you'll gain about, hmmm let me see, about zero pounds.<P>The a/c stuff however is quite heavy.||||reg||1
Z||000012||HYPTNOTiSE||05-23-2000||08:34 AM||||I heard if you take the fan belt, and fan off the front of the motor, you not only gain horsepower but save weight.  I took that, my power steering pump, and my smog pump off my fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000013||1FST2M6||05-23-2000||08:43 AM||||well there really isn't anything you can ditch that is really heavy and not nice to have... A/C, the fan in the trunk that usually doesn't work, the heat sheilds, the motor lift brackets, The steering damener, the windshield waser bottle and its parts.. all the heat holding insulation in the engine bay, the carpet padding/sound deadening material, get Corbeau Lightweight seats, the seat belts for 4-5 point harness. the rear speakers... the intake resonator box... all weight that you don't have to have... ||||reg||1
Z||000014||bwaits||05-23-2000||06:02 PM||||Do you think the car would have a problem with over heating?<P>Why is the formula lighter?||||reg||1
Z||000015||stimpy||05-23-2000||08:08 PM||||Welcome to the forum George Ryan! It is good to have another Okie on the list. I will give ya a heads up, people talk about stereos here. Big speakers and everything! If you can put up with that, then you may enjoy yourself.  <BR>If anyone is unfamiliar with Mr. Ryan's rep, he has a BAD-ASS quad 4 SCCA solo car and is the motivating influence of the Fiero Club of Tulsa and the SCCA chapter in Tulsa. Here's to you George!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||Patrick's Dad||05-23-2000||11:21 PM||||The GT is heavier because the fastback rear clip is heavier than the notchback's. The vent panels and thedecklid are different, but I don't know if they are any heavier than the notchback's. Maybe it just looks it, but the GT seems to be an inch or two longer in the rear end, which would mean more plastic, as well....<P>The Formula and GT for 88 probably are penalized for the increase in HP (Pre 88 V6's were 135hp, the 88 is 140hp) and the better suspension. I read one of the designers, who said that, of all the mods that they had seen for the Citation/Chevette suspension blend, that what Lotus did equaled or bettered them all. Am I damning the pre-88's? No. My 85 2M4 practically went sideways (lane change) for me in avoiding an accident. I'll still take my Formula, though.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Patrick's Dad||05-23-2000||11:26 PM||||Oh, yeah....<P>Yes the 2.8 in the Fieros is the same, essentially, as in the Cavaliers. The intake (from cleaner to injectors) is completely different, and I wouldn't be too surprised if the cam was just a tad hotter. Beyond that, I'd imagine that they're the same to the exhust manifolds.<P>BTW, the 3.1's and 3.4's are essentially the same engines, with different bore and/or stroke. Again, intakes vary.<P>The Holley throttle body, in particular, is unique to the Fiero. Likely, the Fiero was too low production to tool up for a new TB, so they outsourced it. The Cav's Berrettas, etc. were much larger volume, so GM made the TB's for those.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Sillhouette.com||05-23-2000||11:29 PM||||Your purpose in life for building race cars is to win.  If you win you get sponsors.  The more sponsors you get the more money you make and that is about all of it.  So when considering a race car you must make sure that you don't feel bad when some body panels get cracked during "rubbing" on the track.  You also must make sure that suspension brake parts are cheap and easy to replace.  You will not be happy with the 88 setup.  It is expensive and IMO there is no difference between the older or newer model brake systems.  Same goes for the suspension.  Take everything out of your car carpets plastic panels doors and so on.  You must install a roll cage with a four point harness a fire extingusing system for the engine compartment and plastic windows so they pop out in a collision.  Take the radiator of the front and put it on the back so in the event of a collision you will not be disqualified because of a leaky radiator.  That also saves on weight.  Don't put any fancy gauges you're not there to win a beauty contest your there to win a race.  These are things that I picked up while building the RSM race fiero and racing in solo events for four years now. <P>Hope this helps.<P><BR>Cheers||||reg||1
